          title: "A tyrant's guilty arithmetic",
          body: "Herod Antipas — *tetrarch*, ruler of a quarter, though flatterers said king — hears reports of Jesus and produces the strangest christology in the gospels: **it's John, back from the dead.** The theory is incoherent on its face (John worked no miracles, and Jesus' ministry overlapped John's), but guilt does not do source criticism. Herod has murdered one preacher of righteousness, and now every report of power in Galilee arrives pre-shaped by the thing he cannot stop seeing. It is a precise portrait of how a bad conscience processes the world: not as scepticism — Herod is the opposite of a sceptic, instantly credulous of resurrection — but as *haunting*, in which all new data points back to the buried thing. The narrator lets the theory stand a beat, then unwinds the flashback (vv. 3-12) to show us what Herod is remembering. Worth noticing for this project's purposes: the first resurrection hypothesis in this gospel is proposed by an unbeliever, in terror, about the wrong man — a reminder that in the first century as now, ”someone has been raised from the dead” was the kind of thing frightened people could reach for. What chapter 28 will ask is whether it is also, once, the kind of thing that happened.",
